{"name":"Sweet Surrender","hex":"#c9d0b8","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"surrender","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To give up into the power, control, or possession of another.","To yield (a town, a fortification, etc.) to an enemy."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of surrendering, submission into the possession of another; a feeling of abandonment and resignation; the deliberate cessation of struggle over outcomes.","The yielding or delivery of a possession in response to a demand."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/surrender"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweet Surrender (John Denver song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sweet Surrender\" is a song written and recorded by American singer-songwriter John Denver. It was originally recorded for his 1974 album Back Home Again, but was chosen as the lead single from the concert album An Evening with John Denver. \"Sweet Surrender\" reached No. 13 on the Billboard Hot 100 chart in February 1975, remaining in the Top 40 for eight weeks. It also became his fourth No. 1 on the adult contemporary chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/74/John_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_Surrender_(John_Denver_song)","wikidata":"Q7655527"},{"title":"Sweet Surrender (Sarah McLachlan song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sweet Surrender\" is a song by Canadian singer Sarah McLachlan. It was released in late 1997 as the second single from her fourth studio album, Surfacing (1997). The song peaked at number two in Canada and number 28 on the US Billboard Hot 100. In 2001, a maxi-single with remixes by DJ Tiësto was released peaking at number six on the Billboard Dance Club Play chart, three years after its original release."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3b/Sweetsurrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_Surrender_(Sarah_McLachlan_song)","wikidata":"Q7655528"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/74/John_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg/250px-John_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/74/John_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/74/John_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg/250px-John_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Denver_Sweet_Surrender.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3b/Sweetsurrender.jpg/250px-Sweetsurrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3b/Sweetsurrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3b/Sweetsurrender.jpg/250px-Sweetsurrender.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASweetsurrender.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sweet":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/En-us-sweet.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d7/En-us-sweet.ogg/En-us-sweet.ogg.mp3"}],"surrender":[{"type":"audio/ogg;